  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 337 PM CDT Tue Oct 27 2026\n\nSome weak isentropic lift associated with a shortwave trough \nhas helped maintain the low-level stratus layer across the \narea. As the upper-level jet progresses eastward, upper-level \ndivergence will continue to support these overcast skies through\nthe overnight hours. This cloud cover will limit afternoon \nhighs to the 50s to low 60s. Furthermore, patchy drizzle and \nisolated showers are possible this afternoon through tonight. \nAlong with these dreary conditions, fog is expected to develop \ntonight into tomorrow morning. Recent HREF and REFS guidance \nindicate roughly a 50-80% chance of visibilities falling below \n1/2 mile tonight, primarly along and west of a line from \nCambridge to Fullerton.\n\nAfter this upper-level disturbance moves off, a weak upper-level\nridge will build into the region Wednesday into Friday. This \npattern will allow for some sunshine and slightly warmer \ntemperatures to build back into the area, with highs in the \n70s tomorrow and decreasing into the 60s to low 70s Thursday \nand Friday. However, rain chances reenter the forecast late\nThursday evening through likely Saturday afternoon (though \nthere is a low chance of lingering rain into Halloween evening) \nas a shortwave trough moves overhead. \n\nHowever, the break from precipitation won't last long, as the\nlarger-scale trough moves into the region Sunday into Monday. \nWhile there is some uncertainty on the exact timing and strength\nof this system, model guidance currently shows a Shift of Tails\n(SOT) value over 1 for some areas. This guidance indicates that\nat least 10% of model guidance is forecasting an event beyond\nlocal records; corresponding to approximately 10% of model \nguidance having at least 1 inch of rain, with locally higher \namounts possible. Later forecasts will continue to monitor \ntrends in the timing and rainfall accumulation.",
